//
// PROBE: RFC-0003 §S1's terminator rule, from both sides.
//
// `name`, `atom`, `ptr`, `stack` and `command` require *trailing* whitespace
// (reference/bund_language_parser/bund.pest:26-30), so a program ending in a
// word does not parse. Every one of the reference's parse sites appends `\n`
// to compensate — `bundcore_eval.rs:8`, `helpers/eval.rs`,
// `bund_interpreter.rs:29`, `debug_debug.rs:52` — and the library entry point
// `bund_language_parser/src/compile.rs:15` does not, so it fails on exactly the
// input the other four are working around.
//
// S1 admits end of input as a terminator instead, which is what the append
// achieves, and drops the append. The accepted language is unchanged, and this
// probe pins both halves of that claim:
//
//   - a word at end of input parses (the line below with no trailing space),
//   - a closing brace is still not a terminator, which is why `{ 1 println}`
//     fails — that half cannot be probed here, because a golden of a parse
//     error captures the oracle's ~/.cargo path (F66). It is pinned by test in
//     crates/bund2-syntax/src/lib.rs.
//
"probe: a word at end of input" println
1 2 +
debug.display_stack
